    "topic_id": 37595,
    "poster_id": 651,
    "post_rendered": "I think it is <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/6/b7616a230c77f0d9a577e5bca01aa06f1c35c457.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$17$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"18\" height=\"12\" >,</span> however I havent checked all the cases, what one should try is to look what pairs <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/8/8e8da0aef2d19017da7471378386d61620f288f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a,b)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" > modulo 16, give <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/3/2135a47e8054757e0ebd40aa647d76cf3abf07cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3^a-2^b$\"  width=\"54\" height=\"15\" > a multiple of 17, and show using factorizations, modulo 5, modulo 11, modulo 16, or more, to show they have more factors or the equivalence is not truth!",
    "post_canonical": "I think it is $17$, however I havent checked all the cases, what one should try is to look what pairs $(a,b)$ modulo 16, give $3^a-2^b$ a multiple of 17, and show using factorizations, modulo 5, modulo 11, modulo 16, or more, to show they have more factors or the equivalence is not truth!",
